# Hypothesis Testing & Causal Evidence Architecture

> *"A theory that cannot be falsified is not science — it's storytelling."* — Karl Popper

---

## Objective

Most applied analytics stops at **estimation**: computing a mean, fitting a regression, reporting a coefficient. This project goes one step further — into **falsification**.

Using the Lalonde (1986) experimental dataset, I operationalized the scientific method to adjudicate between two competing causal narratives: *did job training actually move the needle on earnings, or is the observed difference just noise?* The core epistemological pivot is from "what is the effect?" to "can we statistically rule out the possibility that there is no effect at all?"

This distinction is not academic. In production ML systems, the inability to falsify a hypothesis is the root cause of data dredging, p-hacking, and the deployment of models that perform in backtests and fail in markets.

---

## Technical Approach

**Dataset:** Lalonde (1986) Experimental Subset — a randomized control trial measuring the impact of vocational job training on 1978 real earnings (`re78`).

**Parametric Test — Welch's T-Test (Signal-to-Noise Framework)**

- Framed the t-statistic explicitly as a **Signal-to-Noise Ratio**: the Average Treatment Effect (ATE) divided by the standard error of that estimate.
- Used `scipy.stats.ttest_ind` with `equal_var=False` (Welch's correction) to account for heteroskedasticity between treatment and control groups — a more defensible assumption than pooled variance when group sizes or variances differ.
- Applied a significance threshold of α = 0.05 to **control the Type I error rate**, ensuring that the probability of a false positive (incorrectly rejecting a true null) was bounded before any data was examined.

**Non-Parametric Validation — Permutation Test (10,000 Resamples)**

- Earnings data is right-skewed and heavy-tailed — a known violation of the normality assumption underlying parametric tests. To stress-test the T-test result, I ran a `stats.permutation_test` with `n_resamples=10,000`.
- The permutation test builds an **empirical null distribution** by repeatedly shuffling treatment labels, simulating the world where training has zero effect. The observed ATE is then evaluated against this distribution.
- Convergence of the parametric and non-parametric p-values confirms the result is **robust to distributional assumptions** — not an artifact of Gaussian approximation.

---

## Key Findings

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Mean Earnings — Treated | $6,349 |
| Mean Earnings — Control | $4,554 |
| Average Treatment Effect (Signal) | ~+$1,795 |
| Welch's T-Test P-Value | < 0.05 |
| Permutation Test P-Value | Consistent with T-Test |
| Decision | **Reject H₀** |

The null hypothesis — that job training has no effect on earnings — was rejected via **Proof by Statistical Contradiction**. The observed lift of ~$1,795 is unlikely to be attributable to chance under the null distribution.

---

## Business Insight: Hypothesis Testing as the Safety Valve of the Algorithmic Economy

Modern data pipelines are extraordinarily good at finding patterns. They are considerably worse at distinguishing **genuine signal from spurious correlation**. As datasets grow larger, the risk paradoxically increases: with enough features and enough compute, you can always find a statistically significant relationship — even in pure noise — if you look hard enough. This is the core problem of **data dredging**.

Rigorous hypothesis testing — specifically, pre-committing to a null hypothesis, a test statistic, and a significance threshold *before* running the analysis — is the institutional check on this failure mode. It forces the analyst to take a falsifiable position and accept the cost of being wrong.

In the context of asset management and equity research, this discipline is existential. A spurious factor in a quant model doesn't just underperform — it can introduce correlated tail risk that only manifests under stress. The same logic applies to algorithmic hiring, credit scoring, and any decision system where a false positive has asymmetric real-world consequences.

Hypothesis testing, properly applied, is not a statistical formality. It is the **epistemological safety valve** that separates evidence-based inference from pattern-matched overfit.

